		{
			"id": "eTEBtgagiT_gAXqA_",
			"description": "Flamer",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Standard Astra Militarum and Astartes squad flame weapon, used throughout the fiction to clear trenches, bunkers, hab-blocks and tunnel networks of infantry, xenos broods and heretics where a bolter or lasgun would need a clean line of sight to every target; universally shown as short-ranged but able to reach around corners and into cover that stops bullets.\n\nDesign: No anchor exists for flame weapons in the framework, so this entry is built from GURPS's own area/cone attack design language (B412) rather than from a catalogue item: an \"always hits inside the cone, no Acc\" ranged attack with a burning-target follow-up per the Fire rules (B433). Base damage (4d burn) and cone length (5 yards) were set to comfortably kill an unarmoured man and threaten flak on the initial hit at the flak DR this entry was first built against; against the current flak (DR 30) and mesh (DR 36) anchors, two rounds later, 4d burn (avg 14) now penetrates neither (0% of hits by exact dice distribution) — the initial cone hit is no longer a real armour threat at all, only an unarmoured-target killer, and the real killing power comes entirely from repeated, DR-ignoring burning damage once a target catches fire, more true now than when this entry was written. That follow-up is deliberately left unscaled and instead gated by a new sealed-armour exemption (see the Hand Flamer's design note): flame weapons are feared by infantry in any armour short of sealed power armour, and should do next to nothing to a battle-brother who keeps his helmet locked, matching how the fiction never shows a flamer meaningfully hurting a sealed Astartes except by setting his surroundings alight. TL is set at 8, well below the TL10 the framework gives most standard Imperial small arms, because a pressurised-fuel-and-igniter weapon is genuinely simple compared with a lasgun or bolter; LC2 (rather than the low LC4 real stub-tech gets) reflects that the Imperium restricts flame weapons for what they do and who is trusted to carry them, not for how sophisticated they are - a deliberate case where TL and LC diverge that departs from the framework's usual TL-implies-LC pattern, worth flagging as a judgment call rather than a strict anchor.",
